Not sure if it's the same issue, but a similar problem occurs with this simple using the `?.` operator:

```
interface Bar {
  id: string;
  name: string;
}

interface Foo {
  bar: Bar | null;
}

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  standalone: true,
  template: `
    @if (foo.bar?.id) {
      {{ foo.bar.name }}
    }
  `,
})
export class App {
  foo: Foo = {
    bar: {
      id: 'a',
      name: 'A',
    },
  };

  test() {
    if (this.foo.bar?.id) {
      console.log(this.foo.bar.name);
    }
  }
}
```

The `test()` method compiles fine, but the equivalent code in the template doesn't compile, with the following error message:

```
NG1: Object is possibly 'null'. [plugin angular-compiler]

    src/main.ts:19:17:
      19 │       {{ foo.bar.name }}
```

Here's a stackblitz reproducing the issue: https://stackblitz.com/edit/stackblitz-starters-kw7tq8?file=src%2Fmain.ts

